<p><hr align=left size=1 noshade width="100%"><p>
<h1><a name="J-section">J</a></h1>


<p><b><a name="julia_set">Julia Set</a></b> &nbsp; &nbsp;
A <a href="glossary-S.html#set">set</a> of <a href="glossary-C.html#complex_number">complex numbers</a> that do not <a href="glossary-D.html#diverge">diverge</a> if
<a href="glossary-I.html#iterate/iterative">iterated</a> an infinite number of times via a simple equation.  The
points form an extremely complex <a href="glossary-F.html#fractal">fractal</a>. There is an
<a href="glossary-U.html#uncountable_infinity">uncountable infinity</a>  of Julia sets, each of which
corresponds to a particular complex number that appears as a constant
in the iterative procedure.  Julia sets are similar to
<a href="glossary-C.html#co-recursively_enumerable_(co-re)">co-recursively enumerable</a> sets because only points that are not a
members of the set can actually be identified as such.  All of the
Julia sets are related to the <a href="glossary-M.html#mandelbrot_set">Mandelbrot set</a>.  



